  "text": ".WriteHeader Block 1\n짬The cube has vertices $(\\pm 1,0,0)$, $(0,\\pm 1,0)$, $(0,0,\\pm 1)$.\n짬The point P lies on the edges of the cube, so $x,y,z \\in \\{-1,0,1\\}$.\n짬Let's suppose that $x$, $y$, and $z$ are all odd. In this case, the distance between P and the center of the cube is 2, and therefore the sphere cannot be tangent to the axes.\n짬On the other hand, we can imagine that $x$, $y$, and $z$ are all even.\n짬In this case, let's consider that x and y, and y and z are both odd, while z is even.\n짬In this case  $P$ lies on the cross-section of the cube with a plane $x+y+z=4$.\n.ToolStripItem Block 2\n짬Let $q$ be the center of the sphere. Then the distance between $q$ and P is the distance between $q$ and the midpoint of the edge of the cube containing P. \n짬This distance is given by: $d=q-x+y+z-2$.\n.ToolStripItem Block 3\n짬Consider the vertex (0,0,1) of the cube. It is known that: $d= |x|+|y|+|z|-2$.\n.ToolStripItem Block 4\n짬This always gives us: $d= |x|+|y|+|z|-2$.\n.ToolStripItem Block 5\n짬Since $x$, $y$, and $z$ are all odd, it is verified that:\n짬$|x|+|y|+|z|=|x|+|y|+|z|-2+2x+2y+2z-2=4$.\n.ToolStripItem Block 6\n짬This implies that the center of the sphere is $\\sqrt{2}$ units away from the midpoint of the edge containing P.\n.ToolStripItem Block 7\n짬We can establish that the distance between the center of the sphere and the midpoint of the edge containing P is always equal to the distance between the center of the sphere and the vertex. Indeed: \n.ToolStripItem Block 8\n짬  $d(q,P)=d(q,Q)$, where $q$ is the center of the sphere and $Q$ is the midpoint of the edge containing P.\n.ToolStripItem Block 9\n짬  $4=d^2=2(q+1)^2=((q+1)^2-q^2)=((q+1)+q)((q+1)-q)=2(q+1)$.\n.ToolStripItem Block 10\n짬  $1=q+1$ and $0=q$.\n.ToolStripItem Block 11\n짬  Therefore the center of the sphere is always at the origin, and its radius is square root two.\n.ToolStripItem Block 12\n짬 A surface that is tangent to two parallel lines, with a distance between the lines, is a circle.\n.ToolStripItem Block 13\n짬This circle, which is tangent to the two parallel lines, has center on their middle line, and its radius is equal to one half the distance between the two lines.\n.ToolStripItem Block 14\n짬We can prove that it can be constructed for any two parallel lines in the three-dimensional space, and for any distance.\n.ToolStripItem Block 15\n짬Since we found a point on the surface of the cube where the sphere is tangent to two different lines, namely the x-axis and the y-axis, it must be the center of the sphere, and its radius is $\\sqrt 2$.",
